 G_1(\Phi(p),\Phi(q))
       =J(p)G_2(p,q)J(q)^*\qquad(p,q\in W,\ p\ne q).$$

Suppose $O_i\Subset D_i$ are open sets and every $V_i$-valued solution $w$ of $\mathsf L_iw=0$ on $D_i$ satisfies $$\label{cross:interpolation-input}
 \|w\|_{L^2(O_i)}
 \le C\|w\|_{L^2(S_i)}^{\gamma_i}
          \|w\|_{L^2(D_i)}^{1-\gamma_i},
 \qquad 0<\gamma_i<1,\qquad \gamma_1+\gamma_2>1.$$

We will use the following temporary metric comparison: $$\label{sph:smallness}
 H_8(y):=\sum_{|\alpha|\le8}|\partial^\alpha h(y)|
       \le\varepsilon r_\delta(y)\qquad(y\in Y).$$ Coordinate norms in this definition are uniformly equivalent to the reference-metric norms. This condition will later be improved and then removed by a continuity argument.

Use the reference metric and normalized inverse matrices from <a href="#sph:normalization" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sph:normalization">[sph:normalization]</a>. The harmonic-frame convention <a href="#bdy:harmonic-normalization" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bdy:harmonic-normalization">[bdy:harmonic-normalization]</a>, with the multipliers $c_j$ in <a href="#sph:frame-normalization" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sph:frame-normalization">[sph:frame-normalization]</a>, gives equations whose second-derivative coefficient matrices are positive definite: $$\label{ang:frame-equations}
 \mathcal L_1=a^{ij}\partial_i\partial_j+B_1^i\partial_i,
 \qquad
 \mathcal L_2=Q^{ij}\partial_i\partial_j+B_2^i\partial_i.$$ The $B_j^i$ are complex $2\times2$ matrices acting on columns from the left. There is no zeroth-order term because each column of the defining frame $F_j$ is harmonic. All coefficients have uniform smooth bounds on the fixed coordinate neighborhood for sufficiently small spatial dilations. Suppress the dilation and shrinking parameters for now, and write $$r=r_\delta,\qquad f_*=\log r,\qquad
 \beta=|df_*|_\gamma,\qquad f_i=\nabla_i f_*.$$ The profile bounds supplied by Lemma <a href="#sph:profiles" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sph:profiles">11</a> have the form $$\label{ang:profile-bounds}
 \beta\ge\beta_0,\qquad
 \operatorname{Hess}_\gamma f_*\le-c_0\beta\gamma,\qquad
 |\nabla^j f_*|\le C_j\beta^j\quad(1\le j\le8).$$ The lower threshold $\beta_0$ can be increased by increasing $p_*$. Throughout this section we impose $$\label{ang:smallness}
 \sum_{|\alpha|\le8}|\partial_y^\alpha h|\le\varepsilon r,
 \qquad r\beta^2\le\varepsilon.$$ The first is the bootstrap assumption of Section <a href="#sec:spheres" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:spheres">4</a>; the second is ensured by the choice of radius amplitude.

