outputs.exact_statement:
There are compact connected smooth extensions $N_j$ of the original manifold, with nonempty smooth boundary, and a common connected open exterior cap $E\subset N_j^\circ$, attached through a nonempty open patch $P\Subset\Gamma$, such that the extended metrics and connections agree on $E$. For each $f\in C_c^\infty(E;\mathbb C^2)$, the solutions $L_{j,D}^{-1}f$ agree on $E$. The Dirichlet Green matrices, normalized using metric volume, satisfy $$\label{bdy:common-green}
 G_1(x,y)=G_2(x,y)\quad(x,y\in E,\ x\ne y),
 \qquad G_j(x,y)=G_j(y,x)^*.$$

All matches agree on overlaps and are injective on base points. Their union is a unique maximal match $(\Phi,J)$ on a connected open set $W\subset N_2^\circ$. On this set, $$\label{bdy:matched-green}
 G_1(\Phi(p),\Phi(q))
       =J(p)G_2(p,q)J(q)^*\qquad(p,q\in W,\ p\ne q).$$

Let $D_i$ be bounded connected coordinate domains, and let $S_i\Subset D_i$ be nonempty open sets. Suppose a product solution $F$ is given consistently on $$(D_1\times S_2)\cup(S_1\times D_2),$$ with the sum of its two $L^2$ norms at most $M$. Suppose $O_i\Subset D_i$ are open sets and every $V_i$-valued solution $w$ of $\mathsf L_iw=0$ on $D_i$ satisfies $$\label{cross:interpolation-input}
 \|w\|_{L^2(O_i)}
 \le C\|w\|_{L^2(S_i)}^{\gamma_i}
          \|w\|_{L^2(D_i)}^{1-\gamma_i},
 \qquad 0<\gamma_i<1,\qquad \gamma_1+\gamma_2>1.$$ Then $F$ extends as a product solution to $O_1\times O_2$, agreeing with the two given pieces on their intersections. Its $C^m$ norm on every compact subset of the output product is at most $C_mM$. These constants are uniform when geometric margins, ellipticity, the required coefficient bounds, interpolation constants, and a positive lower bound for $\gamma_1+\gamma_2-1$ are uniform. A specified output order requires only finitely many coefficient bounds.

For the geometry just fixed and all sufficiently small $\lambda$, the profiles <a href="#sph:radius" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sph:radius">[sph:radius]</a> satisfy $$\label{sph:profile-bounds}
 \beta\ge cp_*,\qquad
 \operatorname{Hess}_\gamma f_*\le-c\beta\gamma,
 \qquad |\partial^j f_*|\le C_j\beta^j\quad(j\ge1),$$ uniformly in $0<\delta\le1$. The constants $c,C_j$ are independent of $p_*\ge2$. For fixed $p_*,R_*$, the functions $r_\delta^{1/p_*}$ have a uniform Lipschitz bound. The radii increase strictly with $\delta$, converge to zero where $t\le0$, and obey $$\label{sph:amplitude-smallness}
 \sup_{0<\delta\le1,\ y\in Y}r_\delta\beta^2
 \longrightarrow0\quad\hbox{as }R_*\downarrow0$$ for each fixed $p_*$.

Under the assumptions of Proposition <a href="#sph:continuation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sph:continuation">14</a>, there is a smooth matrix function $\psi=\psi_\delta$ on the unit sphere bundle over $Y^\circ$ such that $$\label{sph:density-representation}
 T_yv=\int_{S_y}\psi(y,\omega)
       v\bigl(\exp_y^\gamma(r(y)\omega)\bigr)\,d\sigma_y(\omega).$$ For every $y_0\in Y^\circ$ and every first harmonic-frame function $v$ defined near $\overline{B_\gamma(y_0,r(y_0))}$, the function $y\mapsto T_yv$ is second harmonic-frame harmonic on a neighborhood of $y_0$. For each pair $(v_1,v_2)$ of first and second harmonic-frame functions defined on the fixed coordinate neighborhoods and agreeing on the known side, $$\label{sph:reproduction}
 \int_{S_y}\psi(y,\omega)\,d\sigma_y(\omega)=I,
 \qquad T_yv_1=v_2(y).$$

Under <a href="#ang:profile-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ang:profile-bounds">[ang:profile-bounds]</a>–<a href="#ang:smallness" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ang:smallness">[ang:smallness]</a>, $$\label{ang:C-expansion}
 \mathcal C_{i,0}^*
 =r^{-1}\mathsf A_i^*+f_i\mathsf B+E_i+F_i,
 \qquad E_i\in r\Psi^1,\quad F_i\in\Psi^0.$$ The normalized remainders $r^{-1}E_i,F_i$ have the allowance $C_j\beta^j$ through six base derivatives. Moreover, on rows, $$\label{ang:trace-difference}
 K_i:=\mathcal C_i^t-\mathcal C_{i,0}^*
       \in\Psi^0,\qquad \nabla K_i\in\beta\Psi^0.$$ All constants depend on fixed smooth background bounds, not on the lower threshold $\beta_0$ once $\beta_0\ge1$.

Let $n\ge3$, and suppose <a href="#ang:profile-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ang:profile-bounds">[ang:profile-bounds]</a> holds on a fixed base region with uniformly bounded reference geometry. There are $\beta_0$ and $c>0$, depending only on that geometry and the constants in <a href="#ang:profile-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ang:profile-bounds">[ang:profile-bounds]</a>, such that every smooth, compactly base-supported function with zero fiber mean satisfies $$\label{ang:raising-estimate}
 \|T\varphi\|^2-\|T^\flat\varphi\|^2
       \ge c\,\mathcal N(\varphi)^2.$$

Choose the fixed geometry as in Section <a href="#sec:spheres" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:spheres">4</a>, then choose $p_*$ sufficiently large, $\varepsilon>0$ sufficiently small, and the radius amplitude $R_*>0$ sufficiently small that $r_\delta\beta_\delta^2\le\varepsilon$ for $0<\delta\le1$. For all sufficiently small spatial dilations there is a constant $C$, independent of the dilation and $\delta$, such that $$\label{ang:density-bound}
 \|\chi\psi_\delta\|_{L^2(S_\gamma Y)}\le C$$ whenever $\sum_{|\alpha|\le8}|\partial^\alpha h|
\le\varepsilon r_\delta$ on $Y$. Here $\chi$ is the fixed cutoff from <a href="#sph:cutoff" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sph:cutoff">[sph:cutoff]</a> and $\psi_\delta$ is the left-acting matrix density of Proposition <a href="#sph:transfer" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sph:transfer">15</a>.

With the fixed choices in Lemma <a href="#mat:interpolation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="mat:interpolation">24</a>, there are constants $C$ and $\rho_0>0$, uniform for sufficiently small $\lambda$, such that <a href="#mat:provisional" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="mat:provisional">[mat:provisional]</a> implies $$H_8(y)\le C r_\delta(y)^{3/2}
       \qquad\text{if }y\in K\text{ and }r_\delta(y)<\rho_0.
 \label{mat:improved-h}$$ On the same set, the derivatives of $D$ through order nine are bounded by $Cr_\delta^{3/2}$.
